.TH CSPLIT 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
csplit \- split a file into context-determined pieces
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B csplit
.RB [ \-k ]
.RB [ \-s ]
.RB [ \-f
.IR prefix ]
.RB [ \-n
.IR number ]
.I file
.I arg
.RI [ arg ]\ .\|.\|.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B csplit
reads
.I file
(or standard input, if
.I file
is
.BR \- )
entirely into memory, then writes it out as a sequence of
consecutively-numbered output files, split at the boundaries named by
each
.I arg
operand, in the order given. After the last
.I arg
has been applied, one final piece is always written, running from
wherever the last
.I arg
left off through the end of the file, even if it is empty.
.PP
Each successfully created piece's size in bytes is printed to
standard output, one line per piece, unless
.B \-s
is given.
.PP
Each
.I arg
is matched starting from the current position in the file (just past
wherever the previous
.I arg
left off) and searches forward; it can never match a position before
the current one.
.PP
By default, if any error occurs while processing the
.I arg
operands, every piece already created during this run is removed
before exiting; see
.B \-k
to keep them instead.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-k
Leave any already-created output files in place if an error occurs,
instead of removing them.
.TP
.B \-s
Suppress the per-piece size messages that would otherwise be printed
to standard output.
.TP
.BI \-f " prefix"
Name each created file using
.I prefix
as the filename prefix, instead of the default
.BR xx ,
followed by a zero-padded numeric suffix.
.TP
.BI \-n " number"
Use
.I number
decimal digits, zero-padded, for each created file's numeric suffix,
instead of the default of 2.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I file
The pathname of the file to split. If given as
.BR \- ,
standard input is used.
.TP
.I arg
One of the following forms, applied in the order given:
.RS
.TP
.I line_no
Create a piece from the current position up to, but not including,
line
.IR line_no .
.I line_no
must be a positive line number at or after the current position.
.TP
.I /regexp/[offset]
Create a piece from the current position up to, but not including,
the next line matching the basic regular expression
.IR regexp ,
adjusted by
.I offset
lines if given (a signed decimal integer, written directly after the
closing
.BR / ;
the resulting line must still be at or after the current position and
within the file). The delimiting
.B /
character may appear literally inside
.I regexp
if escaped as
.BR \e/ .
.TP
.I %regexp%[offset]
Equivalent to
.IR /regexp/[offset] ,
except that no output file is created for the skipped section \(em
the current position still advances past it.
.RE
.IP
The
.RB \(dq "{num}" \(dq
repeat-count form (\(dqapply the previous
.I regexp
operand
.I num
more times\(dq) is not implemented, and is refused with a diagnostic.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The file was split successfully.
.TP
.B >0
An error occurred: an invalid option or option argument, a missing
.I file
or
.I arg
operand, an unreadable input file, an
.I arg
that is malformed, out of range, or fails to match, a
.RB \(dq "{num}" \(dq
operand, or a write failure.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR split (1),
.BR grep (1),
.BR sed (1)
